<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Departments and Six Ministries</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sansheng liubu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Departments</a> and Six Ministries was the central administrative structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>. The three departments drafted, reviewed and implemented policy, while the six ministries handled personnel, revenue, rites, war, justice and public works. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty present this system as a core element of Tang governance, and it influenced later Chinese dynasties and neighbouring states.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The system was the main administrative structure of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, replacing the Three Lords and Nine Ministers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Han. Its offices grew out of older posts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a> began as the chief steward for writing, one of six stewards of the Qin palace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu of Han</a> made it the head of six sections of government, which later became the Six Ministries. Emperor Wen of Wei formally created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Central Secretariat</a> to draft edicts and balance the Department of State Affairs, and the Chancellery grew from the Han Court of Attendants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the Tang the post of director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a> was mostly left vacant, and its vice directors, split into left and right to weaken them, ran it; by the mid-Tang it had become a purely executive body. Each ministry was headed by a minister assisted by two vice-ministers and was divided into four bureaus, and the six divisions were repeated at prefectural level. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> also oversaw the Nine Courts and the Three Directorates and was one of the largest employers in the government.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Central Secretariat</a> read incoming material to the throne and drafted edicts, while the Chancellery reviewed them and advised the emperor. From the mid-Tang the Chancellery lost ground to the Secretariat, and by the 9th century it kept only the imperial seals, court ceremonies and altars. Later in the dynasty the academicians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a> took over much of the drafting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> the Secretariat and Chancellery were joined as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretariat-chancellery">Secretariat-Chancellery</a>. The Yuan made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Central Secretariat</a> the heart of government, in control of the Six Ministries. In the early Ming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> executed the chancellor Hu Weiyong in 1380 and abolished the Central Secretariat, placing the Six Ministries directly under the emperor. The ministries continued under the Ming and Qing until new ministries added in 1901 and 1906 made the term obsolete. The system was also used by Balhae and Goryeo and by other states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Three Departments and Six Ministries” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Wen and his successor Emperor Yang pursued centralising reforms, most notably the equal-field system, which aimed to reduce economic inequality and raise agricultural productivity, the Five Departments and Six Boards system that preceded the Tang's <b>Three Departments and Six Ministries</b>, and the standardisation and reunification of the coinage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was built on the structure of <b>three departments and six ministries</b> (sansheng liubu), which became the template for Chinese imperial administration for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
